# 0707 — SERIES 700 MONITORING

## LEGAiSEE COMMANDCENTER KNOWLEDGE SYSTEM

Version: 1.0
Status: ACTIVE GOVERNANCE STANDARD

---

# PURPOSE

The Monitoring subsystem defines the continuous observation, tracking, and reporting mechanisms used to maintain visibility into the operational state of the LEGAiSEE CommandCenter.

Monitoring ensures that every critical component of the system is observable in real time and historically auditable.

Its purpose is to maintain operational awareness, detect anomalies early, and support informed intervention.

---

# CORE PRINCIPLE

A system that cannot be observed cannot be trusted.

Monitoring transforms system behavior into actionable visibility.

It is the foundation of operational control, stability, and accountability.

---

# OBJECTIVES

The Monitoring subsystem shall:

* provide real-time system visibility
* detect failures and anomalies
* track operational health
* support performance analysis
* enable early warning systems
* maintain historical observability
* support debugging and diagnostics
* ensure system accountability

---

# MONITORING DOMAINS

Monitoring spans all layers of the CommandCenter.

---

## Kernel Monitoring

Tracks core system execution including:

* boot processes
* routing activity
* scheduler status
* kernel health
* system uptime
* error states

---

## Engine Monitoring

Tracks intelligence systems including:

* prediction engines
* graph engines
* reasoning systems
* recommendation engines
* memory systems
* processing queues

---

## Data Layer Monitoring

Tracks data integrity and performance including:

* database health
* query performance
* storage utilization
* ingestion pipelines
* graph consistency
* memory store status

---

## Interface Monitoring

Tracks user-facing systems including:

* dashboard responsiveness
* widget behavior
* navigation performance
* visualization rendering
* interaction latency

---

## Workflow Monitoring

Tracks operational processes including:

* excavation pipelines
* investigation workflows
* deployment processes
* validation stages
* background jobs

---

# MONITORING TYPES

---

## Real-Time Monitoring

Continuous observation of system state as it operates.

Focus:

* live metrics
* active processes
* immediate anomalies
* system responsiveness

---

## Event-Based Monitoring

Triggered by specific system events.

Focus:

* errors
* warnings
* state changes
* deployment actions
* security events

---

## Historical Monitoring

Analysis of past system behavior.

Focus:

* trends
* performance history
* failure patterns
* system evolution

---

## Predictive Monitoring

Uses system intelligence to anticipate future issues.

Focus:

* anomaly prediction
* resource exhaustion forecasts
* performance degradation trends
* failure likelihood estimation

---

# ALERTING SYSTEM

Monitoring shall generate alerts when thresholds are exceeded.

Alert categories include:

* critical system failure
* performance degradation
* security violations
* data corruption risk
* resource exhaustion
* workflow failure

Alerts must be prioritized by severity and operational impact.

---

# METRICS COLLECTION

The system shall collect metrics including:

* CPU usage
* memory consumption
* storage utilization
* response time
* throughput
* error rates
* queue depth
* execution latency
* system uptime

Metrics must be consistent and continuously updated.

---

# DASHBOARD INTEGRATION

Monitoring data must be available through:

* system dashboards
* executive dashboards
* operational dashboards
* investigation workspace views
* performance panels

Visibility should be role-appropriate and governed.

---

# LOGGING INTEGRATION

Monitoring and logging systems must be integrated to provide:

* event traceability
* error diagnostics
* execution history
* audit trails
* forensic reconstruction

Logs provide context for monitoring signals.

---

# ANOMALY DETECTION

The system shall identify abnormal behavior including:

* unexpected spikes in load
* degraded performance patterns
* repeated failures
* inconsistent outputs
* unauthorized access attempts

Anomalies should trigger alerts and diagnostic workflows.

---

# ROOT CAUSE SUPPORT

Monitoring must support diagnosis by:

* linking symptoms to sources
* correlating events across systems
* identifying dependency failures
* tracing execution paths
* supporting investigation workflows

Monitoring is not only detection—it is diagnostic infrastructure.

---

# GOVERNANCE

Monitoring shall never:

* suppress critical alerts
* fabricate system states
* ignore anomalies
* bypass logging requirements
* conceal operational failures
* misrepresent system health

Monitoring must remain truthful, complete, and auditable.

